# Big Idea 2: AI Requires Human Judgment

One of the most important understandings students develop throughout Thinking Critically About AI is that AI systems can produce outputs that sound convincing while still being incomplete, misleading, or incorrect.

Students begin recognizing that using AI responsibly requires more than simply accepting the first output generated. Instead, students learn that humans must evaluate, verify, question, and make decisions about how AI outputs should be used.

## What Students Learn

<div style="display:flex; justify-content:space-between; gap:14px; margin:25px 0px;">

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8; padding:18px; border-radius:10px; width:32%; text-align:center;">
    <p style="font-size:16px; font-weight:bold; margin-top:0;">
      Confidence ≠ Accuracy
    </p>
    <p style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:0;">
      Students explore how AI systems can sound extremely confident even when information is incorrect or incomplete.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8; padding:18px; border-radius:10px; width:32%; text-align:center;">
    <p style="font-size:16px; font-weight:bold; margin-top:0;">
      Verification Matters
    </p>
    <p style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:0;">
      Students learn strategies for checking AI-generated information using evidence, trusted sources, and human judgment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="background-color:#F5F7F8; padding:18px; border-radius:10px; width:32%; text-align:center;">
    <p style="font-size:16px; font-weight:bold; margin-top:0;">
      Humans Make Final Decisions
    </p>
    <p style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:0;">
      Students examine when AI can assist humans and when humans need to take a more active role in evaluating outputs.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>

A major instructional goal of this section of the unit is helping students move beyond treating AI outputs as automatically trustworthy simply because they sound polished or professional.

## Across the Unit

As students continue through the unit, they repeatedly encounter situations where AI-generated outputs require human oversight and evaluation.

* **Lesson 5** helps students recognize that even well-designed prompts can still produce unexpected or unhelpful chatbot responses
* **Lesson 6** asks students to evaluate AI-generated feedback and decide which suggestions are actually useful
* **Lesson 7** introduces verification strategies and explores how AI systems can confidently generate false information

Across these lessons, students begin developing habits of questioning, verification, and conditional thinking rather than assuming AI outputs are always reliable.

